{"parentUuid":"2b571b52-690b-421e-a588-1f20df7283d4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014qGKv41yTGDqwQnyq7nUxr","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Cerebras Graphcore Groq combinatorial optimization SAT 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cerebras vs SambaNova vs Groq: AI Chip Comparison (2025) | IntuitionLabs\",\"url\":\"https://intuitionlabs.ai/articles/cerebras-vs-sambanova-vs-groq-ai-chips\"},{\"title\":\"The Token Arbitrage: Groq vs. DeepInfra vs. Cerebras vs Fireworks vs Hyperbolic (2025 Benchmark) | by Aparna Pradhan | GoPenAI\",\"url\":\"https://blog.gopenai.com/the-token-arbitrage-groq-vs-deepinfra-vs-cerebras-vs-fireworks-vs-hyperbolic-2025-benchmark-ccd3c2720cc8\"},{\"title\":\"cerebras-vs-sambanova-vs-groq-ai-chip-comparison-2025. ...\",\"url\":\"https://intuitionlabs.ai/pdfs/cerebras-vs-sambanova-vs-groq-ai-chip-comparison-2025.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Cerebras CS-3 vs. Groq LPU\",\"url\":\"https://www.cerebras.ai/blog/cerebras-cs-3-vs-groq-lpu\"},{\"title\":\"LLM Inference Hardware: An Enterprise Guide to Key Players | IntuitionLabs\",\"url\":\"https://intuitionlabs.ai/articles/llm-inference-hardware-enterprise-guide\"},{\"title\":\"91% of AI papers used NVIDIA in 2024 - Air Street Press\",\"url\":\"https://press.airstreet.com/p/compute-index-2024\"},{\"title\":\"Best LLM inference providers. Groq vs. Cerebras: Which Is the Fastest AI Inference Provider? - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/mayu2008/best-llm-inference-providers-groq-vs-cerebras-which-is-the-fastest-ai-inference-provider-lap\"},{\"title\":\"ALCF Expands AI Testbed: Graphcore Joins Upgraded Cerebras & SambaNova Systems in Spring Deployment\",\"url\":\"https://www.hpcwire.com/off-the-wire/alcf-expands-ai-testbed-graphcore-joins-upgraded-cerebras-sambanova-systems/\"},{\"title\":\"Cerebras at SC22\",\"url\":\"https://cerebras.ai/events/sc22/\"},{\"title\":\"Maintenance Notice: Groq, Sambanova, Cerebras, and Graphcore: May 19 | Argonne Leadership Computing Facility\",\"url\":\"https://www.alcf.anl.gov/support-center/facility-updates/maintenance-notice-groq-sambanova-cerebras-and-graphcore-may-19\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about Cerebras, Graphcore, and Groq as AI accelerator companies. However, the search results do not contain specific information about these companies working together on combinatorial optimization or SAT (Boolean Satisfiability) problems in 2025.\n\nHere's what I found about these companies:\n\n**Company Overview:**\nCerebras Systems, SambaNova Systems, and Groq are three emerging AI hardware companies offering specialized AI accelerators targeting high-performance training and inference workloads in competition with NVIDIA's GPUs.\n\n**Hardware Focus:**\n- Cerebras pioneered wafer-scale processors to maximize parallelism, SambaNova developed a reconfigurable dataflow architecture (RDUs) in integrated systems, and Groq designed a novel streaming Language Processing Unit (LPU) focused primarily on inference.\n- AI-dedicated accelerators include specialty chips from Graphcore, Cerebras, Groq, and SambaNova.\n\n**Current Status:**\nBy 2025, the AI accelerator market is no longer a two-player game, with investments in Cerebras, SambaNova, Groq and others like Graphcore and Tenstorrent revealing a broad bet that specialty hardware will fragment.\n\nHowever, the search results do not mention any specific collaboration between these companies on combinatorial optimization or SAT problems. If you're looking for information about such a partnership or research effort, you may need additional sources.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"68987d41-e3b7-4bf7-8cf9-b3dde2445a8b","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:05.416Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2b571b52-690b-421e-a588-1f20df7283d4","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"73850aed-66ca-495d-9746-5f2c17fb95c1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BwnWdxhJevCi5guJpRJedr","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PDLP GPU LP solver CP-SAT integration 2025 OR-Tools\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"PDLP: GPU & Crossover\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/na50dGHXfLI\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools for JuMP - Optimization (Mathematical) - Julia Programming Language\",\"url\":\"https://discourse.julialang.org/t/or-tools-for-jump/106171\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GPU-Accelerated Optimization with GAMS and NVIDIA cuOpt\",\"url\":\"https://www.gams.com/blog/2025/09/gpu-accelerated-optimization-with-gams-and-nvidia-cuopt/\"},{\"title\":\"PDLP: A Practical First-Order Method for Large-Scale Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.07018v1\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced LP Solving | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/lp/lp_advanced\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Google's PDLP Solver Revolutionizes Linear Programming Problem Solving Efficiency\",\"url\":\"https://quantumzeitgeist.com/googles-pdlp-solver-revolutionizes-linear-programming-problem-solving-efficiency/\"},{\"title\":\"MIP aspects of OR-tools CP-SAT solver. Frederic Didier (fdid@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://www.mixedinteger.org/EUROMIP/2025/slides/frederic-didier.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about PDLP GPU LP solver and CP-SAT integration in relation to OR-Tools:\n\n## PDLP and OR-Tools\n\nPDLP is implemented in C++, available in Google's open-source OR-Tools library, and supports multithreading. PDLP is a practical first-order method for linear programming designed to solve large-scale LP problems. It is based on the primal-dual hybrid gradient (PDHG) method applied to the minimax formulation of LP and incorporates several enhancements including diagonal preconditioning, presolving, adaptive step sizes, adaptive restarting, and feasibility polishing.\n\n## GPU Implementation\n\nWhile the base OR-Tools PDLP implementation in C++ doesn't directly mention GPU support, the development of PDLP has led to further enhancements, including a GPU implementation and incorporation into commercial solvers such as Cardinal Optimizer.\n\n## CP-SAT Solver\n\nThe CP-SAT solver is designed for integer programming problems and is generally faster than MPSolver. However, the search results don't show direct integration between PDLP GPU solvers and CP-SAT in 2025.\n\n## Related Discussion\n\nThere has been discussion about whether PDLP with crossover would be useful for CP-SAT to solve LP relaxations.\n\nThe search results don't contain detailed information about specific 2025 developments regarding PDLP GPU LP solver integration with CP-SAT in OR-Tools.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"29a5d83e-1a84-4884-a467-87f2d215e498","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:05.956Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"73850aed-66ca-495d-9746-5f2c17fb95c1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"74081bee-90bf-4e80-97cc-b274f6c872cf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FMF28JTXUbqCpEv7LHJ9WJ","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Apple M4 Ultra OR-Tools CP-SAT benchmark ARM performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Apple M4 10 Core Benchmark\",\"url\":\"https://www.cpubenchmark.net/cpu.php?id=6040&cpu=Apple+M4+10+Core\"},{\"title\":\"Question - Incredible Apple M4 benchmarks... | AnandTech Forums: Technology, Hardware, Software, and Deals\",\"url\":\"https://forums.anandtech.com/threads/incredible-apple-m4-benchmarks.2619241/\"},{\"title\":\"Apple M4 (10 cores) Processor - Benchmarks and Specs - Notebookcheck Tech\",\"url\":\"https://www.notebookcheck.net/Apple-M4-10-cores-Processor-Benchmarks-and-Specs.835975.0.html\"},{\"title\":\"Apple M4 - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M4\"},{\"title\":\"Apple M4 Max CPU Faster Than Intel and AMD in 1T/nT Benchmarks | TechPowerUp\",\"url\":\"https://www.techpowerup.com/328404/apple-m4-max-cpu-faster-than-intel-and-amd-in-1t-nt-benchmarks\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarks for Apple M4 Max and M3 Ultra Processors and Apple Silicon - Does It ARM\",\"url\":\"https://doesitarm.com/benchmarks\"},{\"title\":\"Apple M4 chips - PassMark Support Forums\",\"url\":\"https://forums.passmark.com/performancetest/58298-apple-m4-chips\"},{\"title\":\"Apple M4 (10-Core): benchmarks and specs | NR\",\"url\":\"https://nanoreview.net/en/cpu/apple-m4\"},{\"title\":\"Apple vs. Oranges: Evaluating the Apple Silicon M-Series SoCs for HPC Performance and Efficiency\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2502.05317v1\"},{\"title\":\"Apple Chip Comparison (March 2026) M1 vs M2 vs M3 vs M4 Complete Guide\",\"url\":\"https://www.ofzenandcomputing.com/apple-chip-comparison/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about Apple's M4 chip and ARM performance, but the results do not contain specific information about OR-Tools CP-SAT benchmarks on the Apple M4 Ultra.\n\nHere's what I found about Apple M4 ARM performance:\n\nThe Apple M4 is a series of ARM-based systems on a chip made by Apple, part of the Apple silicon series, including a central processing unit (CPU), a graphics processing unit (GPU), a neural processing unit (NPU), and a digital signal processor (DSP). It is Apple's first SoC to reportedly use the ARMv9 CPU architecture, and is based on ARMv9.2a.\n\nRegarding general M4 performance, the M4 competes for the highest-scoring consumer SoC for single-core benchmarks according to various sources such as the Geekbench benchmarking suite and Passmark Software's CPU benchmarks.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain any information about OR-Tools CP-SAT (Constraint Programming - Satisfiability) benchmark results specifically for the Apple M4 Ultra or any ARM performance data for this particular solver. To find this information, you may need to search for academic papers or technical benchmarks from Google's Operations Research team or specialized constraint programming research publications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e2078a62-9cf1-43d7-a9a7-a03b2c2621bd","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:06.460Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"74081bee-90bf-4e80-97cc-b274f6c872cf","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"352a94b8-f799-4a17-b2d1-35944e278fc7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K8JJP6X15VVxzxAYAjM8Bs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"NVIDIA Blackwell RTX 5090 GNN SAT solver acceleration 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"V1.1 NVIDIA RTX BLACKWELL GPU ARCHITECTURE Built for Neural Rendering\",\"url\":\"https://images.nvidia.com/aem-dam/Solutions/geforce/blackwell/nvidia-rtx-blackwell-gpu-architecture.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GeForce RTX 5090 Graphics Cards | NVIDIA\",\"url\":\"https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/geforce/graphics-cards/50-series/rtx-5090/\"},{\"title\":\"GeForce RTX 50 series - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeForce_RTX_50_series\"},{\"title\":\"RTX 5090 5080 Blackwell GPUs for AI Generation 2025 - Apatero Blog | Apatero Blog - Open Source AI & Programming Tutorials\",\"url\":\"https://apatero.com/blog/rtx-5090-5080-blackwell-ai-image-video-generation-guide-2025\"},{\"title\":\"NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090 and the Age of Neural Rendering at Hot Chips 2025 - ServeTheHome\",\"url\":\"https://www.servethehome.com/nvidia-geforce-rtx-5090-and-the-age-of-neural-rendering-at-hot-chips-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"How GeForce RTX 50 Series GPUs Are Built to Supercharge Generative AI on PCs\",\"url\":\"https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/rtx-ai-garage-blackwell-nim-blueprints-pc/\"},{\"title\":\"NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5090 Review: Pushing Boundaries with AI Acceleration - StorageReview.com\",\"url\":\"https://www.storagereview.com/review/nvidia-geforce-rtx-5090-review-pushing-boundaries-with-ai-acceleration\"},{\"title\":\"Nvidia GeForce RTX 5090 Founders Edition review: Blackwell commences its reign with a few stumbles | Tom's Hardware\",\"url\":\"https://www.tomshardware.com/pc-components/gpus/nvidia-geforce-rtx-5090-review\"},{\"title\":\"Unlocking the Power What the RTX 5090 Can Do in 2025\",\"url\":\"https://heqingele.com/blog/rtx-5090-900-1g144-2530-000-gaming-ai-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"RTX 5090 AI Benchmarks: Blackwell for Consumer Inference - Code Worm\",\"url\":\"https://www.codeworm.dev/2026/03/rtx-5090-ai-benchmarks-blackwell-for.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the NVIDIA Blackwell RTX 5090, though the specific combination of GNN (Graph Neural Networks) and SAT solver acceleration wasn't explicitly mentioned in these results. Here's what I found:\n\n## NVIDIA RTX 5090 Blackwell Architecture\n\nThe RTX 5090 was announced at CES 2025 and released in January 2025, based on NVIDIA's Blackwell architecture featuring fourth-generation RT cores for hardware-accelerated real-time ray tracing, and fifth-generation deep learning–focused Tensor Cores.\n\n## AI and ML Performance\n\nThe GeForce RTX 5090 is powered by the NVIDIA Blackwell architecture and equipped with 32 GB of super-fast GDDR7 memory. For AI workloads that can use the new FP4 number format, Nvidia claims the 5090 can be up to three times as fast as the 4090.\n\n## Tensor Core Improvements\n\nRTX Blackwell adds new support for FP4 and FP6 Tensor Core operations, and the new Second-Generation FP8 Transformer Engine. These GPUs were built to accelerate the latest generative AI workloads, delivering up to 3,352 AI trillion operations per second (TOPS).\n\nHowever, the search results did not contain specific information about GNN (Graph Neural Network) or SAT (Boolean Satisfiability) solver acceleration on the RTX 5090. If you need more targeted information about those specific use cases, I could perform an additional search.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"11faa409-ecfb-4169-9c85-343e807e1f4e","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:07.237Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"352a94b8-f799-4a17-b2d1-35944e278fc7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"3f07bfbc-aac3-483c-8917-40ed54b9ec7a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Rq4B78gXRSWcnCkvurf4ez","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Intel AVX-VNNI AMX SAT solver propagation SIMD acceleration\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Advanced Vector Extensions - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Vector_Extensions\"},{\"title\":\"AVX-512 - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AVX-512\"},{\"title\":\"CPU Instruction Sets for LLM Inference: AVX, AMX, SME vs GPUs | Cortensor\",\"url\":\"https://docs.cortensor.network/technical-architecture/ai-inference/cpu-instruction-sets-for-llm-inference-avx-amx-sme-vs-gpus\"},{\"title\":\"Intel® Intrinsics Guide\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/intrinsics-guide/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"intel-intrinsics-guide/index.html at master · idma88/intel-intrinsics-guide\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/idma88/intel-intrinsics-guide/blob/master/index.html\"},{\"title\":\"AVX-512 Vector Neural Network Instructions (VNNI) - x86 - WikiChip\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikichip.org/wiki/x86/avx512_vnni\"},{\"title\":\"Intel Releases ISPC 1.24 With New AVX-VNNI & AVX512-VNNI Targets - Phoronix\",\"url\":\"https://www.phoronix.com/news/Intel-ISPC-1.24-Released\"},{\"title\":\"Deep Learning with Intel® AVX-512 and Intel® DL Boost\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/guide/deep-learning-with-avx512-and-dl-boost.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solved: What is the different between AVX-VNNI and AVX512-VNNI ? - Intel Community\",\"url\":\"https://community.intel.com/t5/Processors/What-is-the-different-between-AVX-VNNI-and-AVX512-VNNI/m-p/1460968\"},{\"title\":\"What’s New in LLVM for 4th Gen Intel® Xeon® & Max Series CPUs\",\"url\":\"https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/whats-new-in-llvm-for-4th-gen-intel-xeon-processor.html\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about Intel AVX-VNNI, AMX, and related SIMD acceleration technologies:\n\n## AVX-VNNI (Vector Neural Network Instructions)\n\nAVX-VNNI is a VEX-coded variant of the AVX512-VNNI instruction set extension, limited to 256-bit vectors and does not support additional features of EVEX encoding. These extensions allow for supporting VNNI and IFMA operations even when AVX-512 is not implemented in the processor. AVX-VNNI is found with Intel Alder Lake and newer along with upcoming AMD Zen 5.\n\n## AVX-512 VNNI\n\nThe AVX512 VNNI x86 extension extends AVX-512 Foundation by introducing four new instructions for accelerating inner convolutional neural network loops. VPDPBUSD fuses VPMADDUBSW, VPMADDWD, and VPADDD and VPDPWSSD fuses VPMADDWD and VPADDD.\n\n## AMX (Advanced Matrix Extensions)\n\nThe search results mention AMX as an instruction set family available alongside VNNI, with variants including AMX-BF16, AMX-INT8, AMX-TILE, AMX-FP16, and AMX-COMPLEX. Performance comparisons show LLaMA-3 3.2B INT8 → ~57 tokens/sec (AMX on) vs ~28 t/s (AMX off), and generic prompts reaching ~100 t/s with AMX vs ~25 t/s baseline.\n\nHowever, the search results do not contain specific information about SAT solver propagation acceleration using these technologies. You may need to search for more specialized research papers or technical documentation specifically focused on SAT solver optimization with SIMD acceleration.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d6db26f3-2e23-4f83-ab12-435b5ec8e410","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:09.739Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3f07bfbc-aac3-483c-8917-40ed54b9ec7a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
