{"parentUuid":"8a2dad36-e8d6-4cd2-a74e-277ff57e7902","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01KXcq7Kh69ugWZ2YYnF1JB3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Jc6iZGXDP81bdTwSQfNSvS","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"NeuroSAT\" GNN graph neural network SAT solver 2025 industrial benchmark"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1608,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4970,"cache_read_input_tokens":35968,"output_tokens":629,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4970},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1608,"output_tokens":629,"cache_read_input_tokens":35968,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4970,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4970,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaosUvPjVroSbBxhTPJe7","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b4c569c8-6b58-417a-a2de-3526d6ff4f2c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:32.818Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bb75c921-e79f-40c0-bd8c-1c54ad9a9087","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HBuF7xdDW18FRXvNTgacT1","type":"tool_result","content":"# TurboSAT Paper: Key Details Missing\n\nBased on the abstract provided, here's what we **can** and **cannot** determine:\n\n## What We Know:\n- **Hardware**: \"NVIDIA DGX GB200 node\" was used for prototyping\n- **Speedup**: \"runtime speedups up to over 200x\" versus state-of-the-art CPU solvers\n- **Benchmark Type**: \"public satisfiable benchmark problems from the SAT Competition\"\n\n## Critical Information NOT Disclosed in This Abstract:\n\n- **Single-threaded baseline comparison**: The speedup is against \"a state-of-the-art CPU-based solver\" but doesn't specify whether that's single-threaded or multi-threaded\n- **Specific industrial benchmarks**: No details on whether results cover industrial CDCL instances or are limited to particular problem classes\n- **Consumer GPU compatibility**: GPU memory requirements and consumer GPU viability are not mentioned\n- **Code release status**: The abstract contains no information about open-source availability\n\n## Bottom Line:\nThe abstract is promotional rather than comprehensive. To answer your detailed technical questions, you'd need to access the full 7-page paper itself (available via the PDF or HTML links on the arXiv page)."}]},"uuid":"40dfcafa-1818-4e47-98eb-f5ef7e6244ad","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:33.849Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bb75c921-e79f-40c0-bd8c-1c54ad9a9087","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"4af45e43-99d8-4297-84e2-f60d773a637c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SjaMYaAP4Npc6qumVuaRem","type":"tool_result","content":"# cuOpt: Latest Version & Hardware Requirements\n\n## Latest Version\nThe current release is **v26.06.00** (as indicated by the version badge and pip/conda installation commands).\n\n## Hardware Requirements\n\n**GPU Architecture:** \"Volta architecture or better (Compute Capability >=7.0)\"\n\nThis supports both consumer and datacenter GPUs meeting this specification. The RTX 4090/5090 (Ada/Blackwell architectures) easily exceed these requirements, as do H100/B200 datacenter GPUs.\n\n**Other Requirements:**\n- CUDA 12.0+ or 13.0+\n- NVIDIA driver ≥525.60.13 (Linux) / ≥527.41 (Windows)\n- Python 3.11-3.14\n- Linux x86_64 or aarch64 (Windows via WSL2)\n\n## Supported Problem Types\n\n**Supported:**\n- Linear Programming (LP)\n- Mixed Integer Linear Programming (MILP)\n- Quadratic Programming (QP)\n- Vehicle Routing Problems (VRP, TSP, Pickup-Delivery)\n\n**Capabilities:** \"Near real-time solutions for large-scale LPs with millions of variables and constraints, and MIPs with hundreds of thousands of variables.\"\n\n## Not Explicitly Mentioned\nThe documentation doesn't specifically address combinatorial optimization, scheduling, or 2D placement problems. These may fall under MILP/VRP categories but aren't highlighted as distinct supported problem classes."}]},"uuid":"250adffa-c852-4780-8d40-01864e568403","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:37.079Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4af45e43-99d8-4297-84e2-f60d773a637c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c182a4ba-ce35-420e-85fe-6f0b51038866","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011stgfZPjddMiHG3kK9YiPw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"CP-SAT\" Python ARM macOS M3 M4 ortools binary wheel performance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"CP-Sat: Python 3.7.2, ortools 7.0 wrong optimization result · Issue #1162 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/1162\"},{\"title\":\"Add official Python 3.14 wheels (Linux/macOS/Windows) for OR-Tools · Issue #4859 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/4859\"},{\"title\":\"Installing OR-Tools for Python from Binary on MacOS | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/install/python/binary_mac\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Release Notes | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/support/release_notes\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"Introduction - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/\"},{\"title\":\"or-tools/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.py at stable · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/blob/stable/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.py\"},{\"title\":\"Basic Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about OR-Tools and CP-SAT solver for Python. Here's what I discovered:\n\n## Installation on macOS\n\nOR-Tools for Python has been tested on MacOS 13.0.1 (Ventura) with Intel or M1 processors, and you can install OR-Tools by running `python3 -m pip install ortools` in your terminal.\n\n## Binary Wheels\n\nThe main format for Python modules is now wheel files, and to install OR-Tools for python from pypi, you just run pip install ortools.\n\n## CP-SAT Solver Details\n\nThe CP-SAT solver is designed for integer programming problems and is generally faster than MPSolver. CP-SAT is one of several solvers available in OR-Tools alongside Routing, Linear Solver, and others.\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific performance benchmarks comparing M3 and M4 ARM processors on macOS, or detailed technical performance comparisons for the binary wheels on these architectures. For more specific performance data on ARM-based Macs (M3/M4), you may want to check the OR-Tools GitHub repository's issues and discussions, or run your own benchmarks on those systems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f28ca58e-1138-44e8-9999-28560ca5bf1d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:38.031Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c182a4ba-ce35-420e-85fe-6f0b51038866","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"8a2dad36-e8d6-4cd2-a74e-277ff57e7902","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y8VJDQXPJ4M32eBRS4H2hN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PDLP cuPDLP GPU LP solver consumer GPU RTX 4090 benchmark 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"cuPDLPx: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.14051\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLP+: A Further Enhanced GPU-Based First-Order Solver for Linear Programming\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.14051v1\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - COPT-Public/cuPDLP-C: Code for solving LP on GPU using first-order methods\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/COPT-Public/cuPDLP-C\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - MIT-Lu-Lab/cuPDLPx: A GPU-Accelerated First-Order LP Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/MIT-Lu-Lab/cuPDLPx\"},{\"title\":\"How to Get Started with NVIDIA cuOpt\",\"url\":\"https://www.akamai.com/blog/cloud/2025/oct/how-to-get-started-nvidia-cuopt\"},{\"title\":\"Nvidia RTX 4090 - GPU benchmark - UserBenchmark\",\"url\":\"https://gpu.userbenchmark.com/Nvidia-RTX-4090/Rating/4136\"},{\"title\":\"cuPDLP.jl: A GPU Implementation of Restarted Primal-Dual ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2311.12180\"},{\"title\":\"MAY 2025 HiGHS Newsletter 25.0\",\"url\":\"https://highs.dev/assets/HiGHS_Newsletter_25_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"PassMark - GeForce RTX 4090 - Price performance comparison\",\"url\":\"https://www.videocardbenchmark.net/gpu.php?gpu=GeForce+RTX+4090&id=4606\"},{\"title\":\"Getting Started with NVIDIA CuOpt\",\"url\":\"https://atalaykutlay.com/getting-started-with-cuopt.html\"}]\n\nI've found comprehensive information about PDLP and cuPDLP GPU LP solvers from 2025. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview\n\ncuPDLPx is a further enhanced GPU-based first-order solver for linear programming. cuPDLPx is a GPU-accelerated linear programming solver based on a restarted Halpern PDHG method specifically tailored for GPU architectures.\n\n## Performance Improvements\n\nOn the MIPLIB relaxations, cuPDLPx achieves a 2.5×–5× speedup over cuPDLP, while on Mittelmann's benchmark set, the gains are even larger, reaching 3×–6.8× speedups across different tolerances and presolve settings.\n\n## Key Features\n\ncuPDLPx incorporates a number of new techniques, including a new restart criterion and a PID-controlled primal weight update.\n\n## Hardware Considerations\n\nNVIDIA GPU with CUDA 12.4+ is required, along with CMake (≥ 3.20), GCC, and NVCC build tools. The search results include benchmarks on GPUs like the RTX A6000 and H100, though specific RTX 4090 benchmarks weren't prominently featured in these particular 2025 results.\n\n## Recent Developments\n\nThe solver has implementations in multiple languages, including a cupdlpx Python package supporting NumPy and SciPy, and CuPDLPx.jl providing a JuMP/MathOptInterface wrapper.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"53ba8943-6b77-40da-badd-9ab11967de3c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:40.188Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8a2dad36-e8d6-4cd2-a74e-277ff57e7902","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b4c569c8-6b58-417a-a2de-3526d6ff4f2c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d2d8c1c5-cf59-474f-8fab-14abba897913","agentId":"a6e2f3150e1184d9d","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Jc6iZGXDP81bdTwSQfNSvS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"NeuroSAT\" GNN graph neural network SAT solver 2025 industrial benchmark\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SAT-GATv2: A Dynamic Attention-Based Graph Neural Network for Solving Boolean Satisfiability Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-9292/14/3/423\"},{\"title\":\"1 Introduction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2504.01173\"},{\"title\":\"GRAPH RECURRENT ATTENTION NETWORKS FOR SOLVING ...\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/pdf/2506e22bfc230841780b46c4c63e8f68ce59b1c8.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Predicting Propositional Satisfiability Based on Graph Attention Networks | International Journal of Computational Intelligence Systems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s44196-022-00139-9\"},{\"title\":\"GraSS: Combining Graph Neural Networks with Expert Knowledge for SAT Solver Selection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11024v1\"},{\"title\":\"NEURAL NETWORKS AND THE SATISFIABILITY PROBLEM A DISSERTATION\",\"url\":\"https://users.cs.utah.edu/~tch/notes/PSSAT/IR/SAT/SAT-NN.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Goal-Aware Neural SAT Solver Emils Ozolins, Karlis Freivalds, Andis Draguns,\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.07162\"},{\"title\":\"Graph neural network based time estimator for SAT solver | International Journal of Machine Learning and Cybernetics | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13042-024-02327-9\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7959828/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - souravskr/MS-Project: Experiments on Neural Network-Based SAT Solver with different instances.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/souravskr/MS-Project\"}]\n\nI found several relevant results about NeuroSAT and GNN-based SAT solvers. Here's what I discovered from the search:\n\n## NeuroSAT: The Foundation\n\nNeuroSAT was the first model to utilize GNNs for SAT classification, and GNN architecture variants are derived from the NeuroSAT architecture which demonstrated the possibility of using GNNs for SAT solving. By modeling SAT formulas as literal-clause graphs and employing MPNNs, NeuroSAT demonstrates the potential of GNNs in capturing structural relationships within SAT formulas.\n\n## 2025 Developments and Improvements\n\nRecent work in 2025 shows significant progress in this area. SAT-GATv2 achieves an accuracy improvement of 1.75–5.51% over NeuroSAT on challenging random 3-SAT(n) instances. Additionally, GranSAT achieves the best performance in all but 3 out of 49 train-test pairs for easy instances, and 1 out of 49 pairs for hard instances, with huge performance improvements of up to 60% in some cases.\n\n## Industrial and Benchmark Applications\n\nG4SATBench was developed to benchmark various GNN architectures (GCN, GGNN, GIN) across different graph representations and supervision objectives. The field has moved beyond pure prediction to practical applications: Graph-Q-SAT leverages reinforcement learning and GNNs to optimize branching, reducing the number of iterations for solving instances.\n\nThe search results show that GNN-based SAT solvers like NeuroSAT have evolved significantly, with newer models incorporating advanced techniques and achieving substantial improvements in both academic and potentially industrial benchmark settings.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"fd8c2a2e-bc5c-4974-985c-e8e20f31dce9","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:52:41.377Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b4c569c8-6b58-417a-a2de-3526d6ff4f2c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
